WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.000
Does "Fast X" mark a return&nbsp;
to form for the "Fast" saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.200
or is it way too over-the-top? Early reviews&nbsp;
are in, so gather your found family and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.320
check out what the critics are saying as&nbsp;
the end of the road officially begins.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.040
The “Fast &amp; Furious” franchise has evolved&nbsp;
from a slightly over-the-top series about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.880
street racing and organized crime to&nbsp;
a spectacle of vehicular destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.680
skyscraper stunts, and even a trip to outer&nbsp;
space in a rocket-powered Pontiac Fiero. Dom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.580
Toretto stomped so hard he brought down&nbsp;
a crumbling parking garage, Luke Hobbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.260
redirected an ice-skating torpedo with his arms&nbsp;
— the action is absurd, and that’s the appeal.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.549
"That was awesome!"

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:36.060
Of course, this legacy of uninhibited&nbsp;
ridiculousness means that "Fast X" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.880
some almighty expectations to&nbsp;
live up to. The good news, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.060
is that the franchise hasn't run out of&nbsp;
gas in the action department just yet.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.540
Early reports indicate that the&nbsp;
latest sequel is non-stop carnage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:48.480
with critic and podcast host&nbsp;
Nathan Flynn posting on Twitter,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.860
"FAST X is a fun time at the movies delivering&nbsp;
all the high-octane action global spectacle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.640
and grumbled Vin Diesel monologues about&nbsp;
family you come to expect from this franchise."

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.500
That said, bonkers action is meaningless unless&nbsp;
there's a central conflict between Dom's ragtag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.940
group of heroes and a formidable villain. Jason&nbsp;
Momoa's Dante has been tasked with bringing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.140
inferno in "Fast X," and early reactions have&nbsp;
heaped praise on the actor's performance.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.180
"I'm Dante. Enchanté."

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.360
Momoa reportedly relishes his&nbsp;
villainous role in "Fast X."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.140
The actor believes that he got stuck playing&nbsp;
goody-two-shoes characters for far too long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.740
so he told Variety that he welcomes the&nbsp;
opportunity to portray a compelling bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.680
guy in a high-profile project.&nbsp;
His passion shows on the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.780
and fans have praised Dante’s wickedness on social&nbsp;
media. Critic and podcaster Matt Rorabeck tweeted,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.320
"Jason Momoa is the exact amount of&nbsp;
unhinged needed in the latest chapter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.060
of this live-action Saturday morning cartoon&nbsp;
soap opera. I live, laugh, love the melodrama."

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.340
Some viewers think Momoa is&nbsp;
the highlight of the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.620
movie. Critic and journalist Scott Menzel wrote,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.020
"Jason Momoa steals the show over and over again."

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.060
Writer and critic Kit Stone added,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.100
"Fast X is the best ride you'll take this year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.860
Jason Momoa's peacockish&nbsp;
villainy steals the show."

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.460
Don't expect Dante to be a sympathetic&nbsp;
villain, either. Several early viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.580
compared the character to the Joker&nbsp;
due to his unhinged personality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.280
such as Cinemablend's Eric Eisenberg, who posted,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.580
"[Momoa's Dante is] a gleeful&nbsp;
psychopath, and it's delightful."

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.900
Glowing early reviews aside, the "Fast &amp; Furious"&nbsp;
franchise isn't for everyone. Some people can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.700
look past the unrealistic action setpieces, so&nbsp;
"Fast X" probably won't be met with universal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.920
acclaim. Film critic Emmanuel Noisette found some&nbsp;
scenes too over-the-top to comprehend, writing,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.880
"[Fast X] is on cruise control with&nbsp;
ridiculous stunts and logic-defying action."

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.780
However, the reviewer did at least reserve&nbsp;
some praise for Momoa's villainous performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.320
noting that the actor, quote, "was the&nbsp;
saving grace as an entertaining villain."

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.400
On the flip side, some viewers had a blast&nbsp;
with the gung-ho action sequences. "615&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.360
Film" critic Kevin Allen believes that "Fast&nbsp;
X" is the best franchise installment since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.040
"Furious 7," simply due to the absurdity of the&nbsp;
action scenes and the performances of Momoa and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.340
Brie Larson. Additionally, the reviewer&nbsp;
took a moment to praise the filmmakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.360
saying that director Louis Letterier&nbsp;
"maximizes the stakes with solid direction."

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.640
Elsewhere, some early viewers appreciated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.060
self-aware nature of the film and praised&nbsp;
it for not taking itself too seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.200
meaning that it's everything people expect&nbsp;
at this point from a "Fast &amp; Furious" yarn.

